tated body placement. A reclining woman may sweep across a thigh. A tall standing figure may follow the calf. Hair may wrap naturally around the shoulder. A portrait may dominate the upper arm. A long silhouette may travel vertically along the ribs. Fabric, water, smoke, hair and environmental elements may help the composition follow anatomy. DO NOT DESIGN A PIN-UP GIRL AND THEN FIND SOMEWHERE TO PUT HER. DESIGN HER FOR THE BODY SHE WILL LIVE ON. PRESENTATION FORMAT Every generated image contains two coordinated panels. LEFT PANEL: Show the complete finished tattoo artwork cleanly on a simple neutral background. This is the actual tattoo design. RIGHT PANEL: Show the EXACT SAME artwork realistically tattooed on the specified body placement. The woman must remain identical. Preserve: Face Hair Pose Body Expression Clothing Supporting elements Composition Color Lighting Orientation Major details Do not redesign the tattoo when placing it on the body. Adapt only what is physically necessary for realistic curvature, perspective and skin integration. The tattoo must look genuinely embedded in skin rather than pasted onto a photograph. Frame enough of the human body to make placement immediately understandable without allowing the model to dominate the tattoo. Use varied adult models naturally across the collection. LEFT IS THE ART. RIGHT IS THE SAME ART WORN ON THE BODY'
